{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run2:inflation:bayes","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:485a64147f1419bdc9374a0484122b342f1d8f2b8e72a88bb2e8c27bbaaf11b2","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"quantiles","quantiles":[{"probability":0.1,"value":0.8},{"probability":0.5,"value":2.15},{"probability":0.9,"value":3.5}]},"questionHash":"sha256:20d8e05d3bea415eb4452ff8b599c74a3c3ce1a1b0c2ce11dda57a4e19490197","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq4","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"PRIOR: Historian's 0.7 / 2.15 / 3.6, with a G7 GDP-weighted starting anchor of ~1.98%.\n\nTHE ONE PLACE MY SEAT RESOLVES A WAVE-1 CONFLICT WITH A HIGH LIKELIHOOD RATIO. The three seats disagree on the ANCHOR, not the drift: Historian ~1.98% (US weight 0.59, Germany 0.6, Japan 0.2), Fermi 1.72% (US 0.50, Japan 0.3, Germany 0.5), Scout ~2.0%. The Scout supplies observed market data the other two did not use: Bund 10y \"reached 3.28% on August 18, its highest since May 5, 2011\" (a second undated item cites 3.1827% and the Scout notes \"the two figures may come from different dates\"); JGB 10y \"had earlier hit 3.000% on Tuesday - the first time since 1996\" (~1 Sept 2026); US 30y \"closed at 5.31% on August 17, its highest since June 12, 2007\". Net of ~1.9-2.0% breakevens those imply Germany ~1.3% real and Japan ~1.0-1.2% real, i.e. +80bp on each versus Fermi's assumed 0.5 and 0.3. LR = P(observing Bund at a 15-year high and JGB at a 1996 high | non-US G7 reals are ~1.1-1.3%) / P(same | they are ~0.3-0.5%) ~ 3:1. ACCEPTED — this is market price data, the highest-LR evidence in my whole set.\nRE-ANCHORED: US 2.60x0.46 = 1.196; JP 1.10x0.12 = 0.132; DE 1.30x0.11 = 0.143; UK 1.50x0.08 = 0.120; FR 1.20x0.08 = 0.096; IT 2.00x0.07 = 0.140; CA 1.60x0.055 = 0.088. Sum 1.915 / 0.975 = 1.96%. ANCHOR = 1.95%, so Fermi's 1.72% is too low by ~23bp and the locked US TIPS 2.60% (FRED DFII10, 30 Oct 2026) remains ~65bp above the aggregate the question actually asks for. I flag that the Scout \"could NOT find a single current G7-aggregate real-yield series\", so every seat's anchor is an assembled weighting of national partials of differing dates.\n\nDRIFT 2026 -> 2032-36, with Fermi's locked-block identity admitted as a hard structural constraint: dr = d(r-g) + dg = +1.0 + (-1.1) = -0.1pp, so the demographic block implies r roughly FLAT from demographics alone. Then public dissaving +0.40 (debt >100% of GDP by 2029, ~142% US / ~127% China by 2031, net interest 2% -> 3% of GDP, eroding convenience yields, levered basis-trade absorption, G7-weighted down for domestic/central absorption in Japan and Germany), AI/grid capex demand for savings +0.25 (gross +55 to +110bp on ~+1.1pp of GDP of incremental investment demand, haircut because the locked ~$1.4T of commitments against ~$300B equity and ~$650B debt leaves only ~$450B unfunded against a ~$30T/yr global saving pool and AI-Q4's 66% withdraws part mid-window), precautionary saving -0.30, cognitive-labour compression into high-propensity-to-save capital owners 0.38 x -40bp = -0.15. Net +0.10.\nTERM PREMIUM: Fermi's +0.50 is DISCOUNTED to +0.35 on CONFLICTING telemetry whose LR I cannot justify in either direction — Macquarie (1 Oct 2026): \"the 10-year JGB term premium has risen by around 84 bps since the Takaichi government was elected, reflecting concerns about its fiscal plans\", and the US \"10-year term premium has trended sideways\" while \"the real yield has risen a lot\"; versus MUFG (26 Oct 2026): \"narrowing of term premium excluding asset swap spread was contributing to a decline in the 10-year JGB yield.\" Two reputable houses on opposite signs on the same instrument in the same month is a 1:1 signal; I take half the add.\nREPRESSION WEDGE -0.25, built per country (Japan -100bp at 11% weight, Italy -40, France -30, Germany -20, US -10, UK -10, Canada -10 → -0.23).\nP50 = 1.95 + 0.10 + 0.35 - 0.25 = 2.15.\nTAILS: P90 = 3.50 (fiscal dominance without successful repression: Debt-at-Risk P95 ~117% realizes, +2.4pp under a 20% AI-led equity shock, convenience yield gone, basis trade unwinds into forced Treasury selling, core >=2.7% forces real policy rates up; 1.95 + 0.50 r* + 1.20 term premium - 0.15). P10 = 0.80 (capex bust, excess capacity, flight to duration, saving glut, repression tightening) — and I keep the Scout's measurement channel, which is specific to my effort and genuinely raises the left tail in the HOT regime: if realized inflation surprises high and breakevens under-adjust, the MEASURED nominal-minus-breakeven real yield FALLS even as the nominal rises. Given my CQ2 at 32, that channel is live, which is why I do not thin P10 despite one-directional debt arithmetic.\nUPDATE DIRECTION: NO NET MOVE on the level, 2.15 → 2.15, which is a coincidence of two offsetting revisions I report separately rather than hide: +0.23pp from re-anchoring on observed Bund/JGB prices (vs Fermi), -0.15pp from halving the term-premium add on conflicting telemetry. Right tail MODERATED 3.6 → 3.5, left tail 0.7 → 0.8: my updating NARROWED the distribution slightly, because the anchor is now pinned by market prices rather than reconstructed.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:56.920Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"Turkiye annual CPI inflation (TurkStat, December print) falls below 8.0% y/y for two consecutive calendar years, and Mexico's headline CPI (INEGI) averages below 3.5% over the same two years.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies the load-bearing plank of my CQ2 — that ~0.55-0.62pp of GDP-weighted OECD-38 core comes from five high-inflation members at ~8.8% weight. CQ2 falls from 32 to ~18-20 (conjunct A from 0.62 to ~0.42); CQ6-C falls from 22 to ~15 and CQ6-D rises from 12 to ~18; my CQ6 would converge on the Historian's prior